{"name":"Stoic White","hex":"#e0e0ff","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stoic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Proponent of stoicism, a school of thought, from in 300 B.C.E. up to about the time of Marcus Aurelius, who holds that by cultivating an understanding of the logos, or natural law, one can be free of suffering.","A person indifferent to pleasure or pain."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the Stoics or their ideas.","Not affected by pain or distress."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stoic"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stoicism","blocks":[{"text":"Stoicism is a philosophical movement and practical guide to living, emphasizing daily self-discipline and moral improvement, which originated in the Hellenistic period of ancient Greece and continued well into the Roman Imperial period. The ancient Stoics believed that the universe operated according to reason, or logos, providing a unified account of the world, constructed from ideals of rational discourse, monistic physics, and naturalistic ethics. These ideals constitute virtue, which is necessary for the Stoic goal of 'living a well-reasoned life'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Paolo_Monti_-_Servizio_fotografico_%28Napoli%2C_1969%29_-_BEIC_6353768.jpg/330px-Paolo_Monti_-_Servizio_fotografico_%28Napoli%2C_1969%29_-_BEIC_6353768.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoicism"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg/500px-Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg/1280px-Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg/500px-Detail._Marble_bust_of_Chrysippus._Roman_copy_%281st_century_CE%29_of_an_original_%28c._200_BCE%29._Altes_Museum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chrysip